# Arrays and Loops Guide

Now that you know how to handle clicks and inputs, let's handle **Lists** of data.

## 1. Update HTML (`index.html` or new file)

Add this section:

```html
<hr>
<h2>Todo List (Arrays & Loops)</h2>
<input type="text" id="todoInput" placeholder="Enter task...">
<button id="addBtn">Add Task</button>

<ul id="todoList">
    <!-- Tasks will appear here -->
</ul>

<script src="arrays_loops.js"></script>
```

## 2. The JavaScript Code (`arrays_loops.js`)

Create a file named `arrays_loops.js` (or use internal script) and add this:

```javascript
// A. Create an Array to store our tasks
let myTasks = ["Learn JS", "Eat Lunch", "Sleep"];

let listElement = document.getElementById("todoList");
let addBtn = document.getElementById("addBtn");

// Function to Show (Render) the list
function renderTasks() {
    // 1. Clear the current HTML inside the list so we don't duplicate
    listElement.innerHTML = "";

    // 2. Loop through every task in our Array
    myTasks.forEach(function(task) {
        // Create a new <li> element
        let li = document.createElement("li");
        li.innerText = task;
        
        // Add it to the <ul>
        listElement.appendChild(li);
    });
}

// Initial render to show the default items
renderTasks();

// B. Add new items
addBtn.addEventListener("click", function() {
    let input = document.getElementById("todoInput");
    let newTask = input.value;

    if (newTask !== "") { // Simple validation: don't add empty tasks
        // Add to our Array
        myTasks.push(newTask);
        
        // Update the screen
        renderTasks();
        
        // Clear the input box
        input.value = "";
    } else {
        alert("Please enter a task!");
    }
});
```

## Explanation & "How To"

### Arrays (`[]`)
**Goal:** Store a list of things in one variable.
*   `let list = ["A", "B", "C"];`
*   **`push()`**: Adds a new item to the end of the list.

### Loops (`forEach`)
**Goal:** Do the same thing for every item in a list.
*   `myTasks.forEach(function(item) { ... })`: Runs the code inside `{}` once for "Learn JS", once for "Eat Lunch", etc.

### `innerHTML = ""` vs `appendChild`
*   **`innerHTML = ""`**: Wipes out everything inside an element. We do this before re-drawing the list so we don't see the old items + new items (duplicates).
*   **`document.createElement("li")`**: Creates a fresh HTML tag in memory.
*   **`appendChild(li)`**: Actually puts that fresh tag into the page so the user can see it.

### Pros & Cons of this approach
*   **Pros:** This is the foundation of modern apps (like React, Vue). You have "State" (the array) and "UI" (the HTML), and you sync them.
*   **Cons:** Re-rendering the *whole* list every time you add one item can be slow if you have 10,000 items (but for 50 items, it's instant).
